---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import DocTerminal from '../../components/DocTerminal.astro';
import { implementationNav } from '../../lib/sections';

const runner = `git clone https://github.com/unidpp/unidpp-py
cd unidpp-py

python scripts/eu_profile_report.py
# writes conformance/eu-profile/REPORT.md and REPORT.json
# exit 0: suite ran to completion and both reports were written

python scripts/competitor_report.py
# writes conformance/competitors/REPORT.md and REPORT.json — the
# freeDPP and open-dpp artifacts through the same runner`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Conformance"
  description="Public, reproducible conformance evidence: every example payload printed in EN 18223:2026 run through the UniDPP EU-profile validators — 22 fixtures, 14 pass, 8 fail, 26 error-severity findings recorded as a factual register — and the same pipeline over the published freeDPP and open-dpp artifacts, with the command to run it yourself."
  eyebrow="Evidence — conformance"
  sectioned
>
  <Sidebar slot="rail" heading="Implementation" items={implementationNav} />
  <h1>Conformance</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Conformance evidence for the EU profile, published as a factual register. The
    validators were run against every example payload printed in
    EN&nbsp;18223:2026 — 22 fixtures — and each finding below cites the standard's own
    normative text. The suite is reproducible: the runner command is at the foot of this
    page, and it regenerates and verifies every fixture on each run.
  </p>
  <p class="ledger">
    <span>22 fixtures run</span>
    <span class="ok">14 pass</span>
    <span class="neg">8 fail</span>
    <span>26 error-severity findings</span>
    <span>+ 2 probe checks</span>
    <span>+ 4 info-severity vacancy references</span>
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <h2 id="method-h">Method</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Corpus: clause&nbsp;5.2 compressed JSON examples, Annex&nbsp;A expanded JSON
        examples 1–6, and Annex&nbsp;B XML examples 1–8, extracted verbatim from the
        corrected reissue (2&nbsp;June&nbsp;2026).
      </li>
      <li>
        Validation layers: document-header rules on the payload as printed; EU-profile
        JSON Schemas on the normalized document; EU-profile semantic rules encoding the
        EN's own normative text; neutral-core identifier mapping.
      </li>
      <li>
        Check classes: the runner's registry names four — <code>positive-corpus</code>
        (wire schemas and commitment chains over the ported fixtures),
        <code>audit-negative</code> (defect-register cases fail with precise findings),
        <code>temporal-conformance</code> (every timestamp field against
        ISO&nbsp;8601-1:2019 as impacted by Amd&nbsp;1:2022 — separator typography,
        extended format, second precision, calendar ranges, the end-of-day form, zone
        designators, the decimal sign), and <code>carrier-budget</code> (each fixture's
        canonical serialized size against the ISO/IEC&nbsp;18004 QR capacity tables,
        ported from the CLI).
      </li>
      <li>
        The carrier check is an observation, not a finding: carrier budgets bind Tier-A
        carrier-embedded packs, not served documents, so the size is recorded against
        the QR capacity tables without affecting the outcome.
      </li>
      <li>
        Outcome semantics: <em>pass</em> means no error-severity finding — the payload is
        consistent with the standard's own normative text and sibling examples.
        Info-severity findings are registry-vacancy references and do not affect the
        outcome.
      </li>
      <li>
        A typography-correction pass (curly quotes, dashes inside timestamps) altered
        <strong>0 of 22</strong> payloads; the correction mechanism stays in force and is
        re-verified on each run.
      </li>
      <li>
        Handling: the source is a conversion of a purchased single-user license; the
        fixtures inherit that constraint and are not redistributed. This page records
        findings and citations only.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="register-h">
    <h2 id="register-h">Findings by register class</h2>
    <p>
      Findings are cross-referenced to the DPP corpus defect register, which classes each
      entry by what it is: an inconsistency within one document (A), across documents (B),
        an under-specification — a vacancy — (C), or a new fact discovered by this run (N).
    </p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="register">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Id</th>
            <th scope="col">Class</th>
            <th scope="col">Findings</th>
            <th scope="col">Statement</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A1</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>2</td><td>Granularity printed with an uppercase initial against the normative lowercase enumeration (§4.1.2.2).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A2</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>2</td><td>Status printed with an uppercase initial against the lowercase enumeration (§4.1.2.1).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A3</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>9</td><td>The data-element class name is spelled two ways — the table and example spelling against the prose spelling — in one case within a single sentence (§5.2.6 vs §4.1.2.1).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A4</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>1 <span class="meta">(probe)</span></td><td>A non-ISO&nbsp;8601 dash form in a last-updated timestamp, in the very example that teaches the format; attested for the source PDF and exercised by probe P-A4.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A5</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>2</td><td>Language tags violating the standard's own structure rule — a bare two-letter code, and an unassigned code where Greek is <code>el</code> (Tables 5–6).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A6</td><td>internal inconsistency</td><td>2</td><td>Schema-version placeholders — <code>ENXXX:v1.0</code> and a draft self-reference — where a version grammar is required.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B2</td><td>cross-document inconsistency</td><td>1 <span class="meta">(probe)</span></td><td>Attribute-name drift between the EN and its IDTA implementation (<code>lastUpdated</code> vs <code>lastUpdate</code>); exercised by probe P-B2.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C1</td><td>under-specification <span class="meta">(info)</span></td><td>2</td><td>Dictionary-reference hosts are placeholders; the semantic repository required by clause 4.3 is unspecified.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C4</td><td>under-specification <span class="meta">(info)</span></td><td>2</td><td>Content-specification identifiers are placeholders; identifier space, allocator, and validation undefined.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N1</td><td>new, this run</td><td>1</td><td>Annex&nbsp;A Example&nbsp;2 is not well-formed JSON as printed: misnested, unclosed braces and literal ellipsis placeholders inside a normative annex.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N2</td><td>new, this run</td><td>2</td><td>Dictionary-reference URIs omit the <code>//</code> authority separator after the scheme (Annex&nbsp;A Example&nbsp;3; the sibling Example&nbsp;1 prints the well-formed form).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N3</td><td>new, this run</td><td>1</td><td>The same relative element identifier used by two siblings, violating the uniqueness rule of Table&nbsp;2 (§4.1.2.3).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N4</td><td>new, this run</td><td>3</td><td><code>xsd:float</code> values serialized as JSON strings, contradicting Table&nbsp;7's type mapping and the compressed form of the same data (§5.2.5).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N5</td><td>new, this run</td><td>1</td><td>One class serialized under two different child keys across sibling examples — <code>value</code> vs <code>elements</code> (Annex&nbsp;A Examples 4 and 1; Table&nbsp;4 models the children differently).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>N6</td><td>new, this run</td><td>1</td><td>A trailing comma after the final member makes Annex&nbsp;A Example&nbsp;5 invalid JSON.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">
      Counts cover fixtures and probes. Of the 32 entries: 26 error-severity findings from
      fixtures, 2 probe checks (A4, B2), and 4 info-severity vacancy references (C1, C4).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="fixtures-h">
    <h2 id="fixtures-h">Per-fixture results</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="register">
        <caption>
          Findings are listed by register id; a repeated id is marked with its count. Two
          payloads failed at parse (N1, N6) before schema and semantic checks could run.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Fixture</th>
            <th scope="col">Clause</th>
            <th scope="col">Kind</th>
            <th scope="col">Outcome</th>
            <th scope="col">Findings</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.4-header</td><td>5.2.4</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A1 A2 A6 C4</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.5-dataelementcollection</td><td>5.2.5</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.6-singlevalued-standalone</td><td>5.2.6</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.6-singlevalued-in-complex</td><td>5.2.6</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.7-multivalued-native</td><td>5.2.7</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.7-multivalued-objects</td><td>5.2.7</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.8-relatedresource</td><td>5.2.8</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">cl5-5.2.9-multilanguage</td><td>5.2.9</td><td>JSON, compressed</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example1</td><td>Annex A, ex. 1</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A3 ×4 C1</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example2</td><td>Annex A, ex. 2</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">N1 &mdash; not parseable</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example3</td><td>Annex A, ex. 3</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A3 ×2 N2 ×2</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example4</td><td>Annex A, ex. 4</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A3 ×4 N4 ×3 N3 N5 C1</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example5</td><td>Annex A, ex. 5</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">N6 &mdash; not parseable A5</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexA-example6</td><td>Annex A, ex. 6</td><td>JSON, expanded</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A5</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example1</td><td>Annex B, ex. 1</td><td>XML</td><td class="neg">fail</td><td class="mono">A1 A2 A6 C4</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example2</td><td>Annex B, ex. 2</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example3</td><td>Annex B, ex. 3</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example4</td><td>Annex B, ex. 4</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example5</td><td>Annex B, ex. 5</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example6</td><td>Annex B, ex. 6</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example7</td><td>Annex B, ex. 7</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">annexB-example8</td><td>Annex B, ex. 8</td><td>XML</td><td class="ok">pass</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="meta">
      The failures concentrate in the Annex&nbsp;A expanded-JSON examples and the two
      header examples (5.2.4, Annex&nbsp;B&nbsp;ex.&nbsp;1, which share one header shape); the
      clause&nbsp;5.2 data-element fragments and Annex&nbsp;B XML examples 2–8 pass.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="competitors-h">
    <h2 id="competitors-h">Competitor artifacts through the same pipeline</h2>
    <p>
      The same runner, with no semantic changes, was run over every machine-checkable
      artifact published by the two open-source DPP implementations outside this
      project — freeDPP and open-dpp: seven artifacts in all. Findings cite the
      artifact verbatim; passes would be reported as passes.
    </p>
    <p class="ledger">
      <span>7 artifacts run</span>
      <span class="neg">6 fail</span>
      <span>1 not applicable</span>
      <span class="neg">251 error-severity findings</span>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>freeDPP</strong> publishes its server source and serves both EN&nbsp;18223
      serializations from two live test endpoints; the six captured wire payloads all
      fail, with 251 error-severity findings in the classes this register already
      records — granularity casing (A1), class-name drift (A3), schema-version
      placeholders (A6), duplicate element identifiers (N3), value-data-type mismatches
      (N4), child-key divergence (N5) — plus the temporal finding T7: server-local
      timestamps with no timezone designator, against the UTC-based requirement of
      Table&nbsp;1. The findings are traceable to the published server model, whose own
      comments place the integrity surface after the pending EN&nbsp;18239/18246
      standards.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>open-dpp</strong> publishes its application source; its one
      machine-checkable passport document is an AAS-based fixture without the
      EN&nbsp;18223 header. It is recorded as a coverage observation — a different wire
      format is out of this runner's checkable surface — and no findings are
      manufactured for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="run-h">
    <h2 id="run-h">Run it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      The runner lives in the <code>unidpp-py</code> repository
      (<a href="https://github.com/unidpp">github.com/unidpp</a>). It writes the full
      report — per-fixture findings with verbatim citations and probe transcripts — to
      <code>REPORT.md</code> and <code>REPORT.json</code>, and regenerates and verifies every fixture
      against its raw copy on each run. Findings about the standard under test are report
      content, not harness failures: the standard's own inconsistencies are the evidence
      this register exists to collect.
    </p>
    <DocTerminal
      label="shell"
      meta="unidpp-py repository root"
      ariaLabel="Commands to clone unidpp-py and run the EU-profile conformance suite"
      text={runner}
    />
    <p class="meta">
      Evidence annex for paper&nbsp;4 (P3 deliverable); the catalogue of planned
      contributions is in the <a href="/papers/">papers catalogue</a>, and the
      demonstration runs are on the <a href="/implementation/demos/">demonstrations
      page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .ledger {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    margin: 0 0 2.2rem;
    max-width: var(--prose);
  }
  .ledger .ok {
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .ledger .neg {
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  td.mono {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
